{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-35284","model_call":15,"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1135"},"record":{"query":{"op":"next","qid":"q1135"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_17_203394793#5_224808678","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1135","rank":2,"snippet":"\"headings\": \"Naturopathy Naturopathy Naturopathy Contents History Practice Methods Evidence basis See also: Evidence-based medicine Safety of natural treatments Vaccination Practitioners Licensed naturopaths Education Political activity in the United States Traditional naturopaths Regulation Australia India North America Canada United States Switzerland United Kingdom See also References Further reading External links \" , \"segment\": \"After a period of rapid growth, naturopathy went into decline for several decades after the 1930s. In 1910, the <cover>Carnegie Foundation for the Advancement of Teaching published the Flexner Report, which criticized many aspects of medical education, especially quality and lack of scientific rigour. The advent of penicillin and other \"miracle drugs\" and the consequent popularity of modern medicine </cover>also contributed to naturopathy's decline. In the 1940s and 1950s, a broadening in scope of practice laws led many chiropractic schools to drop their ND degrees, though many chiropractors continued to practice naturopathy. From 1940 to 1963, the American Medical Association campaigned against heterodox medical systems. By 1958, practice of naturopathy was licensed in only five states. In 1968, the United States Department of Health, Education, and Welfare issued a report on naturopathy concluding that naturopathy was not grounded ","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_17_203394793#4_224806610","rank":1,"relevance":2}]},"time":9},"model_calls":15,"document_judgments":10,"relevance_3":2,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
