{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-35284","model_call":28,"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"abraham flexner\" \"carnegie foundation\" \"medical schools\" standards)"},"record":{"query":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"abraham flexner\" \"carnegie foundation\" \"medical schools\" standards)"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_18_37377756#4_43909287","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q1147","rank":1,"snippet":"See also References External links Medical school associations Application services \" , \"segment\": \"Dartmouth opened its medical school as the fourth established in the United States in 1797. The American Medical Association was formed in 1847. In 1876, the Association of American Medical Colleges was formed. Standards for the education provided at medical schools were developed. In 1910, the Flexner Report reported on the state of medical education in the United States and Canada. Written by <cover>Abraham Flexner and published in 1910 under the aegis of the Carnegie Foundation for the Advancement of Teaching, the report set standards and reformed American medical education. This report led to the demise of many non-university based medical schools. </cover>After World War I, the standard practice of completing an internship after medical school became a commonplace requirement to practice medicine, and a list of approved hospitals for the training of interns was complied. In 1928, the AMA published a set of guidelines for residencies. In 1940, the Report of the Commission on Graduate Medical Education was published, which first described the overworking of interns and residents.\" , \"start_char\": 3559.000000 , \"","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_18_37377756#4_43909287","rank":1,"relevance":2}]},"time":143},"model_calls":28,"document_judgments":16,"relevance_3":3,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
