{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-35284","model_call":43,"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1158"},"record":{"query":{"op":"next","qid":"q1158"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_08_604243814#13_1086729515","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1158","rank":3,"snippet":"Education Fund public radio's future \" , \"segment\": \"It would become more exclusive and that was, on the whole, a necessary change. Unfortunately, it did result in a decline in the supply of doctors and that had very long term, unfortunate consequences for America. SIEGEL: So we're witnessing sometime early in the 20th century a change from a well-intentioned, good-natured local physician, who didn't have much training, frankly, to a kind of a trained scientist with a real medical degree. Prof. STARR: That was the vision. That was Flexner's idea that science had to be brought to the bedside. And his report played a very positive role in doing that. SIEGEL: Was the <cover>Carnegie Foundation so influential that a report from an educator who'd been sent around the country to look at medical schools could have that much influence on the way the country trained doctors?\" , \"start_char\": 5455.000000 , \"title\": \"The Flexner Report And Medical Education : </cover>NPR\" , \"url\": \"http://www.npr.org/templates/story/story.php?storyId=122702668\" }"},"time":9},"model_calls":43,"document_judgments":25,"relevance_3":6,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
